  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CO-Sustainable Access To Safe Water</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Somaliland Water Act 2010 (Law No. 49), the Ministry of Water Development (MoWD) is tasked with acting on behalf of the Government of Somaliland in all matters pertaining to the country&apos;s water resources. This includes managing and developing the country&apos;s water resources. MoWD has regional and district coordinators who oversee water-related projects and collaborate with INGOs such as OXFAM. However, weak or insufficient institutional capacity and scarce resources and lack of knowledge and systems increases the difficulty of reliably providing water supply service by the authorities and community-water management committees, which in this case leads to further internal displacement, health problems and malnutrition.
Oxfam has funded over 71 water supply facilities in Somaliland/Somali, including four desalination units. We have constructed a water supply system with over 10km pipeline, linked rehabilitatedwater source with EFSVL team for irrigation purposes and constructed village/pre-urban water supply system within the village with 50m3 water reservoirs. However, all these systems are not functioning as expected. One of the biggest challenges for these water distribution systems is a management problem, minimum government capacity, insufficient community awareness of their rights, and a clear management model with inbuilt  accountability mechanisms to ensure performance and accountability. The water supply management model still follows the same community-managed approach designed for low-cost water technologies like handpumps. In few locations, the private-public partnership is being very successful model (example &#x2013; Lascanood, Borame, Garowe, Burtinle, and many others) and otherlocations, government utility is very successful (example Hargeisa,Burco and other locations). The rural water supply system is becoming vulnerable for abuse of power and communities suffer for lack of sustained water supply system. Rural water supply system relay on humanitarian organizations for maintenance and there is no existing system of accountability of service delivery to their claimholders.

This project aims to come up with a better modality to manage, operate and sustain water to communities in selected locations of Somaliland by improving governments capacity and increasing community awareness and ownership. This will be done through piloting different management approaches and institutionalizing service delivery system with accountability in Oxfam operated areas including desalination units and piped water supply system</narrative>
  </description>
